  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northeast Wisconsin,\nwith high over southern Alberta. Upper level analysis places low\nover Nevada, with split flow over the western CONUS. Over our\narea, west-southwest flow remains in place with widespread cloud\ncover and occasional light drizzle. Some showers continue to\nlift through northwest South Dakota, with a few trying to push\ninto our area. In addition, a few patches of fog with reduced\nvisibility are noted.\n\nFor today, upper low wraps its way over the Arizona/Utah border,\nwhile most of our area remains under the influence of the \nnorthern stream. To start the day, a stray shower or two can not\nbe ruled out along the southern border, with some patchy \nmorning fog remaining possible throughout the area. As the day \ngoes on, aforementioned surface high settles into the Northern \nPlains with skies clearing over our area from west to east. This\nwill allow temperatures to drop off overnight tonight, with \nmost locations reaching or falling below freezing. Therefore, \nexpect freeze headlines will be issued later today to address \nthis.\n\nGenerally westerly flow remains over our area through\nWednesday, keeping conditions over the area dry. On Wednesday \nnight, a surface low passes from Saskatchewan into Manitoba \nsweeping a late night cold front into the area. This may lead to\nsome light rain showers along the South Dakota border \novernight, but the main concern will be stronger winds \ndeveloping on Thursday.\n\nOn Thursday, northwest winds will be on the increase over the\narea in the wake of the cold front, with current guidance and\nforecast soundings suggesting wind gusts to 45 mph possible.\nCooler air pulls into the area, with high pressure then settling\nin overnight, possibly bringing a hard freeze to parts of the\narea.\n\nA warming trend is then on tap through the end of the week as\nridge over the western CONUS pushes over the plains on Saturday\nahead of a notable short wave, with highs possibly pushing into\nthe 70s over parts of our area on Saturday. Wave then pushes \nmainly north of us, with temperatures remaining quite mild for\nthe start of the upcoming week.",
